Subject: [PATCH] ctrl: an Operating Channel Report handled before its Channel Selection Response completes the radio

Each radio's em sends its own Channel Selection Request (its own radio's preferences), and
em_ctrl_t::find_em_for_msg_type hands each received message to the em of the first radio it names,
each em on its own thread. handle_channel_sel_rsp moves a radio from channel_select_pending to
channel_selected, and handle_op_channel_report from channel_selected to configured. An agent that
reports every radio in its Operating Channel Report, the fronthaul first (EasyMesh fixes no order),
has a second radio's response handled on that radio's em and its report on the fronthaul's. When
the report came first, it found the radio still pending and changed nothing; the response then left
the radio in channel_selected with no report to come, until em_config's time limit cancelled it,
marked the radio misconfigured and renewed the agent. Seen three rounds running after a two-radio
pod's boot (13 October); the fourth won the race.

A report while the request is pending now completes the step: the radio's operating channel is what
the step waits for, and the response's code is not used (any code selects). The response checks and
sets the radio's state under the lock the report already holds, so the two cannot interleave.
gen/tests/channel-report-before-response-test.py reproduces the stuck radio with --expect-stuck on a
source without this patch, and runs the two handlers on two threads at once under ThreadSanitizer.

Platform-independent.

Upstream-Status: Pending

diff --git a/src/em/channel/em_channel.cpp b/src/em/channel/em_channel.cpp
index c8b2ba63..00512c31 100644
--- a/src/em/channel/em_channel.cpp
+++ b/src/em/channel/em_channel.cpp
@@ -1687,6 +1687,18 @@ int em_channel_t::handle_op_channel_report(unsigned char *buff, unsigned int len
         if(radio_em->get_state() == em_state_ctrl_channel_selected){
             radio_em->set_state(em_state_ctrl_configured);
             em_util_dbg_print(EM_CONF, "Set em_state_ctrl_configured for radio %s", ruid_str);
+        } else if (radio_em->get_state() == em_state_ctrl_channel_select_pending) {
+            /* The agent sends this report after its Channel Selection Response, but each message
+             * goes to the em of its first radio, each em on its own thread: a report whose first
+             * radio is another one can be handled before this radio's response. The response
+             * then left the radio in channel_selected with no report to come, until em_config's
+             * time limit renewed the agent. A report while the request is pending completes the
+             * step: the radio's operating channel is what it waits for (the response's code is
+             * not used). handle_channel_sel_rsp checks and sets under the same lock. */
+            radio_em->set_state(em_state_ctrl_configured);
+            radio_em->m_chan_req_msg_id = 0;
+            em_printfout("Operating Channel Report for radio %s before its Channel Selection Response: configured",
+                ruid_str);
         }
     } else {
         em_printfout("No EM instance found for radio %s", ruid_str);
@@ -2026,6 +2038,9 @@ int em_channel_t::handle_channel_sel_rsp(unsigned char *buff, unsigned int len)
             dm_easy_mesh_t::macbytes_to_string(ruid, mac_str);
             em_t *radio_em = reinterpret_cast<em_t *>(hash_map_get(get_mgr()->m_em_map, mac_str));
             if (radio_em) {
+                /* the radio's report, handled on another em's thread, may complete it first
+                 * (handle_op_channel_report, under this lock) */
+                std::lock_guard<std::recursive_mutex> topology_lock(g_network_topology_mutex);
                 if((radio_em->get_state() == em_state_ctrl_channel_select_pending) && (response_msg_id == radio_em->m_chan_req_msg_id)) {
                     radio_em->set_state(em_state_ctrl_channel_selected);
                     radio_em->m_chan_req_msg_id = 0;
